%erg 0.1
Title: Prevent next-id cascade from stray high-ID ticket file
Created: 2026-05-29
Author: Claude

--- log ---
2026-05-29T15:10Z Claude created — UX audit finding F03 (verified); one stray file poisons erg new

--- body ---
## Context

The UX audit (0152, PR #174) found that a single stray file matching the
`NNNN-*.erg` pattern with a high ID poisons all future ticket creation.
Reproduced: with a `9999-bad.erg` present, `erg next-id` returns `10000`;
`erg new` then creates `10000-<slug>.erg`, a 5-digit filename that
immediately fails `erg validate` (pattern is `NNNN-slug.erg`, 4 digits).
Every subsequent `erg new` repeats the failure — the store is stuck
producing invalid filenames until the stray file is removed by hand.

Note: test fixture IDs ≥9000 are reserved (STATE.md), so a `9xxx` file is
plausible in real corpora. The interaction between a 4-digit filename
contract and an unbounded next-id is the bug.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/nextid.go` — `cmdNextID` and the ID-scanning logic
- `src/go/new.go` — `cmdNew`, which consumes next-id to name the file
- `tests/test_nextid.sh`, `tests/test_new.sh` — integration tests

## Actions

1. Decide the contract (smallest correct fix; confirm with author if
   ambiguous):
   - Option A: `next-id` ignores files whose numeric ID is outside the
     4-digit range (≥10000) when computing the next ID, OR
   - Option B: `next-id` errors clearly when the highest valid ID is 9999
     (range exhausted) rather than silently rolling to 10000.
2. Ensure `erg new` never emits a filename that would fail `erg validate`
   — if next-id cannot produce a valid 4-digit ID, fail loudly with a
   message naming the offending high-ID file.
3. Keep behavior unchanged for normal corpora (IDs 0001–9998).

## Test

Add to `tests/test_nextid.sh` (red step): place a `9999-bad.erg` in a
temp store and assert `next-id` does NOT return a 5-digit ID:

```sh
echo "stray" > "$TDIR/tickets/9999-bad.erg"
out=$($ERG next-id "$TDIR/tickets")
case "$out" in
    [0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]) pass "next-id stays 4-digit with a 9999 file" ;;
    *) fail "next-id returned non-4-digit '$out' (cascade bug)" ;;
esac
```

The negative control is current behavior (`10000`), which the test flags
— so it catches the wrong implementation, not just a different one.

## Verification

- [ ] With a `9999-*.erg` present, `next-id` returns a valid 4-digit ID
      or fails with a clear range-exhausted message (per chosen option)
- [ ] `erg new` never produces a filename that fails `erg validate`
- [ ] Normal corpora (no stray high-ID file) behave exactly as before
- [ ] New test fails pre-fix, passes post-fix

## Invariants

- Never lose data: do not rewrite or delete the stray file; only change
  ID selection.
- Filename contract (`NNNN-slug.erg`, 4 digits) is preserved.
- `make test` stays green.

## Exit criteria

- A stray high-ID file can no longer make `erg new` produce invalid
  filenames; the failure mode is either avoided or surfaced loudly, and a
  regression test guards it.
